{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310791,"nonce":"UqklbeAHbAwqQXcoK/bqba3c8q8=","title":"M1 \"REGRESSION\" ROOT-CAUSED = CO-RUNNER CONTENTION FRAGILITY, *NOT* THERMAL, *NOT* A CODE REGRESSION (2026-07-12 cont.)","message":"Direct measured resolution of the M1 \"steady 69-70 -\u003e random 30-50\" complaint. THERMAL framing was\nWRONG (user was right to reject it: thermal was always present, can't explain a regression from steady).\n- QUIET BOX, HEAD, --llvm --release, 500-tok long-context: **STEADY 88.8/89.5/91.0/91.1 tok/s** (W8\n  default, ~2.5% spread). That IS the pre-Haxe 80-90 target, by PURE HAXE. NO code throughput\n  regression — pure-Haxe now AT/ABOVE the FFI baseline. Short 120-tok run = steady 82-87 (inflated by\n  short context). The 500-tok number is the apples-to-apples one vs the user's 80-90.\n- THE PERTURBATION = the macOS `Code Helper (Plugin)` (VS Code extension-host / the Claude Code session\n  itself) CPU. Throughput tracks it DIRECTLY: helper idle 0.2-0.7% -\u003e 82-91; helper busy 75-166% (1-1.6\n  cores) -\u003e 31-42. Correlation is clean across ~30 runs. NOT thermal (no ABBA drift), NOT a rayzor knob\n  (spin budget 200k vs 2k vs 20k = IDENTICAL 84-87 on a quiet box). User confirmed by CLOSING VS Code\n  windows -\u003e steady 89-91 (\"had to close windows, never had to before\" = desktop load genuinely rose).\n- REAL rayzor issue (the user's true point): the pool became FRAGILE to load it used to tolerate. HEAD\n  default grabs ALL 8 P-cores (workerCount=perfCoreCount()=8 on this 8P+2E M1 Pro; 7 workers+main=8\n  claimants, ZERO slack) AND tight-spins continuously (200k budget ~1.9ms \u003e every intra-token dispatch\n  gap, so workers HOLD all P-cores for the whole decode step, never ceding). One co-runner then can't\n  borrow an idle core — it PREEMPTS a spinning worker or the MAIN coordinator (which drives dispatch +\n  the busy-wait join) -\u003e ~2x COLLAPSE (89-\u003e37), not a graceful 1/8 loss. macOS can't hard-pin, and\n  spinning workers look permanently busy so the scheduler never rebalances a bad placement.\n- MEASURED TRADEOFF (controlled 1-core `yes` burner + heavy-helper natural load):\n  * W8 (all P): 89 QUIET / collapses (37 heavy helper, ~67 ragged 1-core burner).\n  * W7 (P-1, RAYZOR_HAXE_MATMUL_WORKERS=7): 80.7 QUIET (-9% peak) / stays ~76 (1-core) ~64 (heavy) =\n    GRACEFUL. P-1 protects the main/coordinator thread from being bumped.\n  * Gentle spin (RAYZOR_HAXE_POOL_SPINS=2000) at W8: TIGHTENS variance under load (66-69 vs 56-71), NO\n    quiet peak cost (cedes cores between dispatches so co-runner/FFI borrow them) — weaker than P-1.\n- QoS-INVERSION HYPOTHESIS *DISPROVEN* (I chased it first, was wrong): c1a9784a boosts spawned workers\n  to USER_INITIATED(0x19) while main (bindPerformance opt-in per 023827c1) stays at inherited QoS —\n  looked like an inversion, but A/B RAYZOR_MAC_WORKER_QOS off vs 0x19 = IDENTICAL scatter (QoS is\n  clamped in the ext-host launch band; irrelevant). Reverted the experimental env-gate.\n- FIX DIRECTION (forward, not landed — user to steer peak-vs-robust): (a) load-ADAPTIVE width (8 when\n  quiet -\u003e keep 89 peak; P-1 when a co-runner is detected -\u003e graceful) is the best-of-both but needs a\n  cheap runnable-thread/idle-core sample at init + periodically; (b) simple P-1 default on Apple Silicon\n  = -9% peak always, always robust; (c) gentler idle (event-driven wait / lower spin) to cede cores.\n  Recipe: RAYZOR_HAXE_MATMUL_WORKERS + RAYZOR_HAXE_POOL_SPINS are the live A/B knobs; ALWAYS capture\n  `ps -Ao pcpu,comm -r | grep 'Code Helper (Plugin)'` per run — an active Claude Code session confounds\n  every M1 wall-clock A/B. Use a `yes \u003e/dev/null` burner for a REPRODUCIBLE 1-core co-runner.\n\nBuilt a standalone SpinPool microbench (parallelRows(256) x2000, /tmp/spinasm/Main.hx), dumped\nRAYZOR_DUMP_LLVM_IR opt IR + `rayzor dump --function workerLoop -O 2` MIR side-by-side (darmie's\nsymbol-tracking tip). GROUND TRUTH for the executed tight spin:\n- Atomic.load/store/fetchAdd = BARE LLVM atomics (load atomic seq_cst / atomicrmw) — ZERO calls.\n  Module census: 19 load-atomic / 28 store-atomic / 7 atomicrmw, 0 `call Atomic_load`. The\n  @:native intrinsic path (hir_to_mir ~13183 -\u003e AtomicLoad inst) is real, not a mir-wrapper call.\n- LLVM LOOP-UNSWITCHES the `if(rlx==1)cpuRelax()`: two spin-loop copies. On M-series relaxHint=0\n  the executed copy (opt-IR blocks %54-\u003e%78-\u003e%82-\u003e%54) is CALL-FREE = load-atomic(state) +\n  load-atomic(shut) + spins++ + cmp spinBudget + branch. The cpuRelax call lives only in the\n  rlx==1 (x86) copy (%43-\u003e%47-\u003e%50). So the ~20% tight-spin self-time is genuine memory-bound\n  spinning on 2 shared control cells, NOT hidden call overhead — no codegen fix exists there.\n- MIR-vs-LLVM delta gotcha: the MIR dump retains fn(line,col) DEBUG LINE-MARKERS (fn330026 x53,\n  args = source line/col) that LLVM-opt STRIPS — they do not execute. Always cross-check MIR\n  (source structure + debug probes) against LLVM-opt (what runs). This is why dumps side-by-side.\n- Micro-opt surface that REMAINS (measurement-independent, verify by disasm): shut is checked\n  EVERY spin iter (2 atomic loads/iter) — could check only at phase transitions (1 load/iter);\n  and seq_cst(ldar) state load could relax to acquire/monotonic if the publish handshake allows.\n  Both are off-critical-path (spin is ~20%, mostly off the quiet-box critical path) so low-value.\nRecipe: RAYZOR_DUMP_LLVM_IR=1 -\u003e /tmp/rayzor_llvm_ir_opt.ll; `rayzor dump \u003cf\u003e --function \u003cname\u003e\n-O 2`; JIT inttoptr addrs differ per run so classify calls by SIGNATURE, or use the named MIR.\n\n- SpinPool dispatch-barrier round-trip MEASURED (microbench, 24348 empty parallelRows(32), best-of-5):\n  **0.91us @4 workers, 1.66us @8** -\u003e 81 dispatches/token ~ 0.08-0.13ms ~ 1% of step. Barrier\n  fixed cost is NOT a lever. band_ms ~= 100% of fwd_s on BOTH boxes (M1 6790/6750ms, NUC 6255/6.2s\n  @300tok) -\u003e all step time is inside band bodies: bandwidth-bound compute + worker tail-spin.\n- The 75-80% pool-idle CPU share = workers spinning DURING others' compute + between dispatches:\n  burns CPU but mostly OFF the critical path on a quiet box (NUC hold-fix A/B neutral proves it).\n  On a LOADED desktop the 8 spinners amplify contention/get descheduled at band-open -\u003e M1's load\n  sensitivity. Fused-matmul does NOT reduce dispatch count (24348 identical) and is p50-neutral.\n- Remaining REAL M1 levers: (1) streaming off decode thread (user-measured silent 75-80 vs\n  streamed \u003c70; handoff Channel\u003cString\u003e caveats apply - concrete @:derive(Send) StreamMsg design);\n  (2) load-adaptive spin/worker count for loaded-desktop resilience (quiet-box-verify first);\n  (3) bandwidth micro-opts only after that (already ~llama.cpp band).\n- Microbench footguns hit: Null\u003cInt\u003e-\u003eInt cross-assign garbage bit AGAIN (bugs_null_int_cross_file\n  _assign; +0 workaround) - bug is LIVE and vicious; and worker-summed sink[0] raced (my bench bug).\n\nProcess-CPU attribution (load-INDEPENDENT; profile-feature build, RAYZOR_CPU_PROFILE=1 +\nRAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1 + RAYZOR_DUMP_ALLOC_AT_EXIT=1 + RAYZOR_DUMP_JIT_MAP=1, join CSV vs\n[fn-ptr-llvm] log lines; delay 6000ms skips compile phase):\n- **~75-80% of ALL consumed CPU = SpinPool idle machinery**: workerLoop self ~20-22% (tight spin)\n  + ~52-56% libsystem leaves under workerLoop (syscall tally: gettimeofday, psynch_cvwait,\n  pthread_cond, mach_absolute_time, swtch_pri = timing + park/cond churn + yields).\n- Band-work lambdas ~15-16%; REAL kernels tiny (FlashDecode.bandOneSigned 0.5-0.7%,\n  Q8Cache.append 0.4-0.6%, Q4Matmul ~1%) — kernels are NOT the M1 bottleneck; the POOL is.\n  Audit's \"Bytes accessors 77M/tok\" NOT visible in-profile → deprioritized.\n- Spin default IS wired, lands at the aarch64-macos 200k floor (pool-spin-calib iters_per_us\n  ~107 → ~1.9ms tight spin) — \"budget too small\" FALSIFIED. My SPINS=300000 \"+32%\" / w6 \"+17%\"\n  were single runs under desktop load — SUSPECT, replicate on quiet box before citing.\n- Hold-phase yield/time syscalls batched 1/256 (298b4ddb): NUC-neutral, M1 profile share\n  unchanged → remaining churn likely park/cond cycling + per-iter externs in the spin phase\n  (cpuRelax/Atomic.load inline status UNVERIFIED BY DISASM at HEAD) + possible ITIMER_PROF\n  syscall-sampling bias.\n- Rust FFI WorkerPool threads get SPAWNED and sit parked (dispatch_semaphore) even on the\n  pure-Haxe path (`sample` tree: WorkerPool::new spawns). Old serving config set\n  RAYZOR_WORKERS=1; current scripts don't. Cheap hygiene candidate.\n- NEXT LEVERS (quiet M1 or NUC discipline required): (1) event-driven worker wait to kill\n  park/cond churn; (2) disasm-verify cpuRelax + Atomic.load inlining in workerLoop; (3)\n  workers=6 M-series default A/B; (4) streaming off decode thread (user: silent 75-80 vs\n  streamed \u003c70).\n\n- **SDOT was NEVER emitted in-model at LLVM tier** (disasm: 0 sdot / 496 smlal in the live band):\n  (a) MCJIT EngineBuilder = empty MCPU -\u003e generic armv8.0 ISel; fixed by stamping per-function\n  target-cpu/features attrs (sweep at engine creation + per-fn on Tier-3 path — unstamped fn w/\n  target intrinsic = FATAL cannot-select). (b) attrs alone insufficient: O3 instcombine turns\n  sext(and(x,0xF)) into zext -\u003e USDOT shape -\u003e needs i8mm, ABSENT on M1 (FEAT_I8MM=0) -\u003e smlal\n  soup even on apple-m1. Fix 456521f0: VectorDot emits llvm.aarch64.neon.sdot DIRECTLY on\n  aarch64 (opaque to IR passes); int totals grouping-invariant; cranelift already sdot-grouped.\n  llc A/B on dumped module proved it (apple-m1: 2/165 folded; +i8mm: all).\n  VERIFIED live: 193 sdot / 0 smlal. Decode 38-\u003e46 tok/s (p50 25.25-\u003e19.5) on that fix alone.\n- **Flash port LANDED FOR REAL (13dff852)**: first \"validation\" was VOID — RAYZOR_KV_Q8 never\n  set, F32 FFI flash ran (thread-PC sample caught it; identical-text at temp0 was the tell).\n  Real run surfaced the 5-shape resolution cluster (see bugs_xmodule_resolution_disease_cluster)\n  + the f16 carry decay. After fixes: prefill logits IDENTICAL to plugin arm, first divergence\n  exactly at first flash step, max|dlogit|=0.53, argmax 33/33 @ temp0.\n- **STATE: 800tok sustained 60.6 tok/s p50 15ms (guest flash) vs 56.1/17 (plugin FFI flash) vs\n  35-38 session start.** Guest banded kernel BEATS serial FFI flash. FFI-kernels reference\n  85-90 tok/s; remaining gap ~1.4x. Cold-run FFI ref re-measured 79.7 tok/s p50 10.5 clean-env.\n- Pool determinism (4fad0248): RAYZOR_HAXE_MATMUL presence-vs-value gate (=0 shrank FFI pool to\n  1 = the user's 40\u003c-\u003e80 variance); CpuTopology.perfCoreCount() (hw.perflevel0) -\u003e rust pool\n  P-2=6, guest SpinPool P=8 (was 10 incl E-cores). SpinPool fat-row regime: rows\u003cclaimants*4 -\u003e\n  chunk=1 dispatch (was serial bail) — enables 8-row flash banding; matmul/quantize unaffected.\n- MEMORY *ROOT-CAUSED + FIXED* (681086a5): the +800MB was Q4Matmul's `dScales:Array\u003cFloat\u003e`.\n  Array\u003cFloat\u003e element access on native lowers to a BOXING per-element extern (one heap alloc\n  per read); the matmul hot row reads it bpr× per output row (128256 rows for lm_head) -\u003e vmmap\n  showed 60.4M LIVE small allocs / 945MB MALLOC_SMALL / RSS 2089MB. NOT flash, NOT InsertFreePass\n  (my earlier +0.85GB-blame-flash note was WRONG — flash slightly REDUCES it). Fix = raw Bytes\n  scale buffer via Mem.load/storeF32 (like every other kernel buffer). RSS 2089-\u003e1536MB, within\n  52MB of FFI (1484). Scale now stored f32 (Q8_K on-disk width, more faithful); 41/41 argmax vs\n  FFI. Diagnosis method: `vmmap -summary \u003cpid\u003e` DefaultMallocZone alloc COUNT is the tell — a\n  60M count with bounded-but-high RSS = per-element boxing in a Haxe hot loop.\n- FlashDecode scratch now PERSISTENT on Q8Cache (4cfc595b), sized maxSeqLen once: the `scores`\n  buffer (numQHeads*cacheLen*4) grew with context and was alloc/freed per layer per token =\n  long-context churn. 2000-tok peak 2319-\u003e2127MB, recovers ~1.5GB. maxSeqLen-strided rows;\n  argmax 33/33.\n- THROUGHPUT IS THERMAL, not fixed: 3 cooled-down runs same flags/prompt = 28.8 / 39.5 / 54.0\n  tok/s (monotonic recovery as the box cooled). My \"60.6\" was a cold-machine best; user's \"50\"\n  is a warmer machine; both real. Heat-soak from a session of testing dominates any A/B. Report\n  a RANGE, always cool before quoting. p50 16-27ms across the same runs.\n- User-visible incident: my UNKILLED 8000-token background disasm probe (790% CPU, 12GB) caused\n  their llama-chat-server 90-\u003e27-\u003e21 tok/s \"variance\" report + the 12GB lingering process. ALWAYS\n  timeout-wrap + pkill after probes. Also: llama-chat lingers at exit in __sigsuspend post-[done]\n  (shutdown mis-dispatch family) — zombie source.\n\n- Cold start: before-main LLVM upgrade = +3.0s to first token (5.97 vs 2.96 cranelift-only).\n  darmie: cold start matters; 'warm tier must carry its weight'. Agreed design in\n  docs/plans/HANDOFF-haxe-decode-80-90.md: PATCHABLE CALL TABLE (baseline CallDirect →\n  load-slot + call_indirect; slots filled at finalize; LLVM/beadie swaps write slots →\n  promotion lands MID-RUN incl. via cranelift thunks) + background LLVM compile + baseline at\n  cranelift-\"none\" + prologue hotness counters feeding beadie(warm)/llvm_queue(hot).\n  Touch points scouted: cranelift_backend.rs ~3326 (normal-call func_ref) + emission ~3340+.\n- AUTOVEC: LLVM-tier loop/SLP vectorizers ON but FP reductions CANNOT vectorize — our\n  fast-math deliberately omits reassoc (bit-exactness). Probe: clean raw-f32 sum = fully\n  scalar, 4.2GB/s fadd-latency-bound; accessor loop same speed. Explicit SIMD is THE pattern;\n  optional @:fastMath per-function reassoc opt-in is the autovec lever. Cranelift: never.\n- Sustained state: 40-41.7 tok/s (90323ba7 header+pairing, cd8de0c8 atomic hoist). Per-thread\n  kernel gap vs Rust in-model (13.7 vs 21.7 GMAC/s) = next diagnosable via instruction-level\n  disasm comparison. test_q4km_qmatmul is VACUOUS for nue (self-contained copy).\n\n---\nname: perf_haxe_kernel_pool_cooperation_blocker\ndescription: \"STATE 2026-07-05 end: Haxe decode 164→~65ms/token (15 tok/s) via intrinsics+f16+fusion; kernel fully SDOT/inline (IR-verified). THE remaining blocker to 80-90: TWO POOLS FIGHT — SpinPool workers park in FFI-glue gaps (1.17ms/dispatch wake tax, work is 65-260µs) but longer spin starves the Rust FFI pool (4x worse). Fix = pool cooperation/unification. Measured attributions inside.\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 0e5285ee-bd3c-4d54-a191-d5a96a4ca7c6\n---\n\nState: decode 22.75-23ms p50 (~35-40 sustained), prefill ~20ms/tok (quantize banded 605188d2,\nshared-quantize prefill 12671c88). Budget: matmul band ~17ms = 102ms work @ ~6x eff; FFI 1.5ms.\n1. IN-MODEL PER-THREAD RATE 11.2 vs isolated-bench 16.1 GMAC/s (Rust in-model ~14.8): bench\n   weights were CACHE-RESIDENT across iterations; in-model each weight streams from DRAM once\n   per token. Next: disasm the in-model band (rule) + a DRAM-streaming bench variant to split\n   codegen-vs-memory-bound. If memory-bound → Mem.prefetch intrinsic (llvm.prefetch) on the\n   next block while dotting the current.\n2. PARALLEL EFF 6x on ~10 claimants: chunk-size + claimant-count sweep (use the PROVEN lazy-env\n   pattern in parallelRows — spinBudget's block; getEnv anywhere else in dispatch = trap 132).\nMath: (1) to 14.8 → ~15ms ≈ 63 tok/s; +(2) to 7x → ~13ms; + FFI/alloc trims → 80-90 reach.\nSwiGLU-elementwise-in-Haxe evaluated and REJECTED (serial Haxe slower than the two µs-scale FFI\nkernels; banding it adds dispatch for nothing).\n\n**afeb76f8**: resolve_type_to_class_symbol had NO TypeKind::Optional arm → a method call on a\nreceiver EXPLICITLY typed Null\u003cC\u003e (vs the working ?param sugar) resolved to no class and the\ncall SILENTLY lowered to NOTHING (MIR branch kept only its line marker). sp.parallelRows on\nsp:Null\u003cSpinPool\u003e vanished → pooled kernels wrote zeros → \"garbage logits\"/both corruption\npost-mortems. Repro preserved: /tmp/cap_param2.hx shape; suite test test_null_receiver_method.hx.\nOPEN follow-up: the drop site downstream of a placeholder MethodCall symbol still needs a hard\nE08xx error (no-silent-fallthrough directive) — TAST emits the MethodCall; something in\ntast_to_hir/hir_to_mir eats it without diagnostic.\n**12671c88**: unblocked shared-quantize prefill (runBanded extraction) — prefill quantizes each\nshared activation ONCE per fused group (was 2-3x per layer).\n\n\n- QKV now on the guest fused kernel (5a8c5c41) — beats FFI-QKV under the partition+adaptive pool.\n- Decode budget @22ms: matmul band ~17ms (102ms serial work / ~6x eff) + quantize ~3ms serial +\n  FFI ~1.5ms + glue. The 2x to 80-90 needs ALL of:\n  (1) in-model per-thread rate 11.2 -\u003e bench 16.1 GMAC/s (banded-access cache behavior; 2-row\n      tiling was +17.7% on the Rust Q6 — port it),\n  (2) parallel eff 6 -\u003e 7.5x (parallelize quantize into the band: per-super-block independent;\n      chunk-size sweep),\n  (3) Q6 rate (25% of MACs; native Rust reference is structurally IDENTICAL to our port — no\n      free restructure win; only tiling).\n- Instrumentation NOTE: adding an env-gated print in parallelRows trapped (132) — Sys.getEnv +\n  println in the dispatch path = the bugs_sys_call_in_loop_body family. The pool's band/quant/\n  dispatch CELLS still accumulate; read them via a same-module accessor called from a NON-hot\n  context if needed (not cross-module — mis-dispatch).\n- Q6 native reference: runtime-core/src/quant/sdot.rs dot_q6_k_q8 (marked dead_code/unused —\n  qmatmul_chunk_impl decides the real path) + q6_k.rs simd128; BOTH hsum per 16-weight group\n  like our port.\n\n\n- Runtime WorkerPool defaults to **1 worker under RAYZOR_HAXE_MATMUL** (worker_pool.rs) — its\n  spin-wait workers were the core war. Decode 65 → **27.5ms/token (36 tok/s)**, prefill 0.69 →\n  0.41s/17tok, STABLE even heat-soaked. RAYZOR_WORKERS overrides.\n- **Idle policy is SELF-TUNING (2bd2a734)**: tight-spin (2k, env-overridable) → yield-hold\n  (Thread.yieldNow loop — runnable, no wake tax, cedes cores) → park after 4× the pool's\n  inter-dispatch-gap EWMA (cells 8/9). Measures 23-23.5ms/token heat-soaked, beating BOTH\n  static configs (40k→27.5; 2M→184-255 hot / 23.75 cold). No per-machine constant.\n  Gotcha found: the Haxe method is Thread.yieldNow (yield_now = trap stub, silent 133).\n- Batch rows: 4-wide scalar register tiles (Array\u003cFloat\u003e accumulator writes were per-element\n  runtime calls).\n- Haxe fused QKV measured SLOWER than FFI fused QKV under the partition (29.75-35 vs 23.75-27.5)\n  — GQAttention keeps QTensor.fusedQkvMatmul.\n\nExtracting matmul's banded pass into `runBanded(qw, batch, K, aBase:Usize, bsums:Bytes,\ndScales:Array\u003cFloat\u003e, sp:Null\u003cSpinPool\u003e)` — a VERBATIM code move — produced garbage output\n(prefill logits wrong from token 1). The band closure inside the helper captures the helper's\nparams; matmul's identical closure over LOCALS works. Also: my earlier batch\u003e1 Mem-scratch\ncrash sized scratch via sp.workers() — cross-module SpinPool method calls MIS-DISPATCH (see\nbelow), so that was likely garbage-count → undersized buffer, same family.\n\ntensor_silu 34.6% (5.8ms/run), matmul_qkv_qt_t_f32_threaded 15.8% (fused QKV — kept), flash_attn_decode\n11.9%, tensor_reshape 11.2% (520 calls!), tensor_rope 9.6%, tensor_rms_norm 7%, tensor_free 4.4%\n(5072 calls), tensor_softmax, tensor_add_into, topk_scan, tensor_clone. Plus per-matmul\nTensor.zeros + Bytes.alloc. All µs-scale; the 1-worker runtime pool covers them.\n\n\nDecode step p50 (1B, RAYZOR_HAXE_MATMUL=1, default run = LLVM tier)